uction sewing  and cutting  along the process big garment companies  very often outsource the production  abroad  mostly in asia  the process from the creation of the  product to selling it in shops is called  the supply chain  so how do we make sure that social and  environmental standards as well as basic  human rights are being respected along  the entire supply chain of companies  this is exactly what due diligence is  all about  making sure that companies act  responsibly and giving access to justice  to anyone who has been a victim of a  company's wrongdoings  today eu companies are only encouraged  to behave responsibly but no legal  obligations are imposed on them and this  is exactly why the smd group in the  european parliament started this battle  to know more about it we met the snd mep  lara walters who is in charge of this  file for the european parliament due  diligence is a duty of care essentially  and it's a duty of care for companies  and it's about environment human rights  and governance so ultimately what this  is about is companies investigating  what's going on in their value chains  and no longer saying we didn't know  about  deforestation forced labor  and those kinds of things we don't have  a law at the moment everything that's  there at the moment is voluntary what we  need companies to do is really take  responsibility and proactively go look  at the problems that are most real to  them in their value chains so what we  need to do is really make sure that  there's accountability responsibility  that lies with the company and not make  this into some tick box exercise that  somebody somewhere in compliance is  responsible for what i want is for ceos  of companies board rooms to take  responsibility to know what's going on  and to personally get involved in this  process so we need to make sure that  there's um that there's a a that there's  access to justice we need to make sure  that it's it's it's possible for for  local communities and so forth to take  big companies to court uh we need to  make sure that they're they're  facilitated in doing that if needed um  and we need to make sure that there's  remedy at all at the end of this ideally  of course this law will prevent any  problems  but if problems cannot be prevented then  we need to make sure there's access to  justice and we need to make sure that  there's remedy that's satisfying we had  a huge amount of negotiating to do to  get to this point there was a huge  amount of  um  talking about this almost education i  would say on what are the problems  what's already being done and so forth  for us to be able to get to the  consensus that we reached that really  wasn't easy my role in that was  mediating facilitating and pushing and  and so as the draft person i was of  course very proud that we were able to  get a big majority on this and my role  going forward will be to really get this  law across the line  after the european parliament repeatedly  called for a law on due diligence fought  for by the progressives the european  commission eventually came up with  proposals  but the progressives weren't alone in  this fight  ngos and civil society have been been  vital in raising awareness in bringing  examples of wrongdoing  to our attention and that's helped  hugely with political momentum  i think they need to be involved in the  process of making this legislation to  make sure that we can actually have an  impact on the ground  we had the opportunity to discuss with  one of the co-founders of these ngos  patrick ali global witness is a pioneer  ngo defending human rights and natural  resources through its investigative work  and campaigning the ngo started raising  awareness about the crimes of the khmer  rouge in cambodia and put a spotlight on  the illegal conflict diamonds that  financed bloody wars in western africa  in the 90s  well i think the broad proposal of  a law on due diligence is a global game  changer  having worked in m